WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.160
﻿On June 6, 2021,

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:05.100
Simone Biles won a historic&nbsp;
seventh national title.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.240
And among all the superhuman feats
she’s pulled off in her career,

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.800
like a Yurchenko double pike
in the US Classic,
&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.880
and a triple double at the 2019
US Gymnastics Championships,
&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:16.900
this one is especially important.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:18.812
It’s called a Wolf Turn.

00:00:18.812 --> 00:00:20.599
The Wolf Turn has been around for decades.&nbsp;

00:00:20.793 --> 00:00:24.583
And it seems like recently, almost every gymnast has included it in their routine.

00:00:24.977 --> 00:00:26.538
It’s certainly not an easy skill.
&nbsp;

00:00:26.709 --> 00:00:28.150
Something even Jessica Alba noticed.

00:00:28.337 --> 00:00:29.834
Why do they always do that?

00:00:30.374 --> 00:00:31.774
Because it’s like really hard.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:33.797
So if this turn is so hard,

00:00:34.045 --> 00:00:35.561
how do all these gymnasts pull it off?

00:00:37.888 --> 00:00:40.112
The wolf turn is broken into&nbsp;
four main movements.
&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.560
A gymnast, like Simone,
will get into a squat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.292
position on the beam or the floor,&nbsp;

00:00:44.292 --> 00:00:45.949
with their other leg stretched out.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.694
She’ll then stretch out her&nbsp;
arms and wind them up.
&nbsp;

00:00:49.192 --> 00:00:51.512
Once she finds her balance,
she’ll start spinning.
&nbsp;

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.957
And finally, she’ll stop, and return
to her original stance.

00:00:55.192 --> 00:00:56.800
All while making sure to keep her balance 

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:57.884
and not fall over.

00:00:58.549 --> 00:01:01.765
These four elements rely&nbsp;on a delicate balance of mass and inertia.

00:01:02.083 --> 00:01:04.480
Try to get in the starting&nbsp;
position for the wolf turn.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.835
I think you’re gonna find&nbsp;
it’s extremely difficult.
&nbsp;

00:01:07.043 --> 00:01:10.286
David Young is a physics professor&nbsp;
at Louisiana State University
&nbsp;

00:01:10.543 --> 00:01:13.223
who's studied the physics of&nbsp;
gymnastics and other sports.
&nbsp;

00:01:13.617 --> 00:01:17.520
When she's in that crouch position,
the majority of her center mass is,
&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.430
of course, still kind of in her torso.

00:01:19.582 --> 00:01:21.520
As long as your center of mass

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:26.320
is above a point of support,
that system will be physically stable.
&nbsp;

00:01:26.876 --> 00:01:29.494
If she leans back a little&nbsp;
bit,  she'll fall over.

00:01:29.818 --> 00:01:32.116
Things start to get tricky&nbsp;
as she attempts to spin.&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.680
To figure out what’s happening,
let’s look at her moment of inertia:

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.609
How her mass is distributed&nbsp;
from her axis of rotation.

00:01:38.876 --> 00:01:41.760
In this case, the rotation&nbsp;
axis is a vertical line&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.212
near the center of her body&nbsp;
that passes through her foot.

00:01:44.392 --> 00:01:49.840
When she winds up and starts spinning,
notice how she'll bring her arms kind of in here,

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.042
closer to her body.

00:01:51.263 --> 00:01:53.111
This distributes her body mass differently,

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:56.720
closer to the axis of rotation,
which decreases her moment of inertia.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.140
This means Biles can spin easier and faster.&nbsp;

00:02:00.417 --> 00:02:04.472
And then right when she gets ready to stop, you'll notice that she'll fling her arms out.

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:06.131
Spreading her body mass out

00:02:06.241 --> 00:02:07.862
increases her moment of inertia.&nbsp;

00:02:07.862 --> 00:02:09.440
This makes it harder to rotate,

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:10.996
and helps her stop quickly.

00:02:11.882 --> 00:02:14.021
All these movements&nbsp;require incredible strength 

00:02:14.021 --> 00:02:16.000
with gymnasts using mainly their arm, 

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.232
shoulder, leg and core muscles
to maintain their position.

00:02:19.501 --> 00:02:22.640
And like Jordan Chiles' 2017&nbsp;
Nationals routine shows,
&nbsp;

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:24.663
one wobble, and things fall apart.

00:02:24.663 --> 00:02:26.376
Cannot believe what she just did there.

00:02:26.531 --> 00:02:28.562
There’s a reason gymnasts go through this tricky balance.&nbsp;

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:30.507
They’re chasing high scores.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:35.200
Olympic gymnastics is scored by combining points from two panels:

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.084
Difficulty and Execution.&nbsp;

00:02:37.804 --> 00:02:40.371
Difficulty assigns a point value&nbsp;to the skill itself.

00:02:40.703 --> 00:02:41.772
A simple turn,

00:02:41.772 --> 00:02:44.919
like this single rotation Carly&nbsp;
Patterson did in the 2004 Olympics,

00:02:45.057 --> 00:02:46.831
is valued the lowest of all the turns.&nbsp;

00:02:47.606 --> 00:02:48.478
A triple double, 

00:02:48.478 --> 00:02:51.840
where gymnasts are flipping twice
and twisting three times all in one shot,

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.995
has one of the highest difficulty ratings
of all gymnastics skills.

00:02:55.334 --> 00:02:57.515
And a triple wolf turn is&nbsp;
somewhere in the middle.

00:02:57.515 --> 00:02:59.355
It carries the highest&nbsp;
values of all the turns

00:02:59.355 --> 00:03:02.100
where gymnasts are doing three&nbsp;
revolutions on the beam or floor.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:05.760
Execution scores you on how&nbsp;
well you perform the technique.

00:03:05.926 --> 00:03:09.074
Basically, are you executing properly?
And if not, what did you do wrong?

00:03:09.074 --> 00:03:12.800
Nicole Langevin is a gymnastics judge,
choreographer and consultant.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.360
To truly spin that with proper posture,
proper foot position, proper execution

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.800
and precision of arms
is pretty darn difficult.&nbsp;

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:25.077
First thing we look for is what position is the foot in when&nbsp;they start?

00:03:25.251 --> 00:03:26.727
The support foot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.727 --> 00:03:30.000
is often turned out like a ballerina. 

00:03:30.208 --> 00:03:33.675
So that's how it has to finish at the end.

00:03:34.218 --> 00:03:36.797
And usually it doesn't.
Usually it finishes parallel.
&nbsp;

00:03:37.088 --> 00:03:40.292
Then I want to see that that&nbsp;
extended leg stays extended.
&nbsp;

00:03:40.292 --> 00:03:43.760
There is a deduction in&nbsp;
gymnastics on every single event,
&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.633
and it's called dynamics:

00:03:45.895 --> 00:03:47.934
Doing difficult things and making them look easy.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:51.772
When you're spinning around&nbsp;
going, whoa, with your arms,&nbsp;

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:53.584
that's not very dynamic.&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:54.960
A perfect wolf turn score

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.280
would combine its relatively&nbsp;
high difficulty level

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:59.301
with a flawless execution--

00:03:59.301 --> 00:04:02.168
something only top athletes can pull off.

